From eb85204a62543beed58f777592eea13aa0518d5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Groboclown <groboclown@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:51:13 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] forward: option to refuse secrets in plain http://
 requests

Add `[forward] plaintext_injection = "allow" | "refuse"`. With "refuse",
an absolute-form `http://` request to a ruled host that carries a
`{{seekrit:NAME}}` placeholder anywhere the proxy would substitute it
(path and query, a header value, or the body) is answered with 403 and
never forwarded. The default, "allow", keeps the historical behaviour.

What it prevents:

- A secret crossing the network unencrypted. Through CONNECT the proxy
  always re-originates TLS to the upstream, so an injected value is only
  sent encrypted; an absolute-form http:// request is forwarded as plain
  HTTP, so a secret injected into it is readable on every hop. With
  "refuse", the only way a secret leaves the proxy is over TLS.
- A placeholder slipping past by position. The check scans the same
  three places substitution does, with `substitute::find_placeholder`,
  which uses the substitution engine's own parser so the two can't
  disagree about what a placeholder is.

Unaffected: plain requests without placeholders (forwarded as before),
an unmatched host's requests under `unmatched_host_policy = "tunnel"`
(forwarded verbatim and never substituted, so they carry no secret), and
the reverse proxy, whose upstreams are the operator's own configuration.
Refusals log `reason = "plaintext_injection"` with the secret's name and
count under the `plaintext_refused` outcome.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
